# DESCRIPTION

mali drives an Arm Mali GPU of the command-stream (CSF) generations: it powers the GPU up, resets it, gives it its page tables, boots the firmware of its microcontroller (the MCU) until the firmware answers a ping, and runs clients' command streams on it (THE CSF CAPSET).
devmgr(8) starts it for the CIX Sky1's GPU:

```text
driver mali
class gpu
match acpi CIXH5000
own-mmu
coherence noncoherent CIX runs it on ACE-Lite, uncached (AbyssBSD gpu.md)
power-up graph
power-clocks gpu_clk_core gpu_clk_stacks
reset-pulse 20
firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin
```

The GPU translates through page tables mali writes, so `own-mmu` gives it the BTI of a device behind no IOMMU whatever the IORT names, and the kernel warns of it.
Its `_CCA` says coherent and the GPU isn't, as CIX's own kernel treats it (the display controller doesn't snoop), so the record's `coherence` wins and mali is started with `--noncoherent`.
`vx hardware trust cix-sky1` lists it as trusted with all of memory.

The Sky1's GPU answers nothing until its power-up is done, in this order, and a read of its registers before it stops the machine (an SError, then a power cycle).
devmgr(8) does the first four steps before it starts the driver, since the record says `power-up graph` (driver(6)), each through what owns it:

1. its power domain, SCMI domain 21 (`_DSD`'s `power-domains`), turned on through scmi(3);
2. its clocks `gpu_clk_core` and `gpu_clk_stacks` (`CLKT`: SCMI ids 31 and 32) turned on the same way;
3. its power resource's `_ON`, which devmgr runs;
4. its reset, `gpu_reset` in `RSTL` (index 9 on the S5 reset controller), pulsed through the controller's driver, sky1-reset(3): held for 20 µs and let go, and given 20 µs more (the record's `reset-pulse 20`: the GPU needs the line asserted and let go, not only released).

The driver starts with the GPU on and does the rest, in its own registers, in this order.
A step that fails stops it there: the steps after it are not taken, the status says which failed and why, and the GPU's fault, MCU and interrupt registers at that moment.
The waits are polls of the raw interrupt status with every interrupt masked, each with a limit.

5. Q-channel clock gating turned on, bit 0 of its RCSU's (its first range's) register `0x218`.
6. The probe, from its second range: GPU_ID, the L2, tiler, memory and MMU features, the address spaces, the CSF id, GPU_FEATURES, the thread limits (THREAD_MAX_THREADS, THREAD_MAX_WORKGROUP_SIZE, THREAD_MAX_BARRIER_SIZE and THREAD_FEATURES, whose registers-per-core and tasks-per-core fields are said in the status) and the shader core, tiler and L2 present masks, and the GPU's name as Arm gives it (`Mali-G720-Immortalis`: architecture 12, product 0, ten cores or more, with ray intersection). A GPU_ID of 0 or all ones, or before the 10th architecture, is no GPU.
7. The harvest: the RCSU's word at `0x304` says which shader cores are fused off, and the shader mask is the GPU's own `SHADER_PRESENT` less those (of the part's ten cores, at bits 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 16, 18, 20 and 22). Every later mask is the reduced one.
8. A soft reset, then every interrupt block masked and cleared; RESET_COMPLETED within 100 ms.
9. COHERENCY_PROTOCOL set to ACE-Lite, and read back, with the shareable-cache bit where COHERENCY_FEATURES has it.
10. The system cache's values: SYSC_PBHA_OVERRIDE3 `0x22000000` and SYSC_ALLOC0 to SYSC_ALLOC7.
11. The L2 powered on, slice 0 only (the firmware powers the shader cores and tilers), L2_READY within 20 ms.
12. The MCU's address space: page tables in the GPU's format (AArch64 stage 1, 4 KiB pages and 2 MiB blocks, as many levels as MMU_FEATURES' VA bits need) in write-combining pinned memory, and each of the firmware's sections in pinned memory of its own, write-combining too, mapped at its address with the permissions its flags give. Nothing the GPU reads is cached by the CPU, so the CPU does no cache maintenance, and no section is mapped cacheable on the GPU's side. The initial bytes are copied in and the rest zeroed where the section says so.
13. Address space 0 programmed with those tables (an UPDATE, within 100 ms; its attributes and translation configuration are the non-coherent ones, since the record says `coherence noncoherent`).
14. MCU_CONTROL set to auto, and the firmware's interrupt for the global interface (bit 31 of the job interrupt block) within 1 s.
15. The interface discovered in the firmware's shared section: its version, the CSG and CS slots, each checked to lie inside the section and to agree with the others.
16. The global interface configured (every shader core allowed, the power-off timer at 10 ms and the idle timer at 800 µs of GPU_TIMESTAMP, whose rate it measures over 2 ms, the progress timer, the idle and counter bits) and a ping, which the firmware must acknowledge within 100 ms. The configuration's acknowledge is waited for 100 ms too, but only reported.

Its record names the CSF firmware, `firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin` (driver(6), firmware(6)), so devmgr gives its host that one file at `/lib/firmware/arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin`, read only.
On start mali reads it and checks its header and every section against the format's rules; an image that breaks one is refused whole and the status names the rule.
Sections for the protected mode are left out.
Where the image has no firmware (the cache was empty when the board's image was built), the GPU is probed and the status says `no firmware`.

The GPU is left powered with its firmware running and its address space 0 programmed.
The waits above poll the raw status with every interrupt masked.
Once the boot is done mali serves the capset below, and then unmasks the job interrupt's global and command-stream-group bits (the firmware's events), the MMU interrupt's fault bit of each address space that holds a client's VM, and the GPU block's FAULT, PROTM_FAULT, RESET_COMPLETED and CLEAN_CACHES_COMPLETED.
devmgr(8) grants the host all three of the GPU's interrupts, by their place in its description (the job, MMU and GPU lines, in that order), and mali takes each, served on a thread of its own that runs the scheduler.
A GPU-level fault is read (GPU_FAULT_STATUS's exception and GPU_FAULT_ADDR) and said as an `event:` line, and no reset follows; the completions are cleared (the control steps poll for them); the power-change events stay masked, since the driver polls power.
The scheduler also runs when it asked to be woken (its tick, a submission waiting on a counter) and every 12 seconds for the watchdog's ping.
Where the job or MMU interrupt can't be had, a thread runs the scheduler every 2 ms instead, and `status` says so.
Where only the GPU's can't, its faults are read whenever the other two threads run the scheduler (at least every 50 ms), and `status` says so.

# THE CSF CAPSET

mali is a GPU class driver (class `gpu`): devmgr(8) mounts its tree at `/dev/gpu<n>`, and its `gpu` service is the class's Device (gpu-device-device(5)), which offers one capset, `csf` (0x1000).
`open` on it returns a channel that speaks `todhchai.gpu.csf.Context` (gpu-csf-context(5)), one context to a client, closed with the channel: a context's groups end and its VMs, buffers and counters go.
`info` reports the GPU's registers and the firmware's interface; `limits` reports the thread registers a user-side driver sizes its work from (THREAD_MAX_THREADS, THREAD_MAX_WORKGROUP_SIZE, THREAD_MAX_BARRIER_SIZE and THREAD_FEATURES, as read).
The objects and their limits are panthor's, the Linux driver's, without its ioctls:

: VM
  A GPU address space: 32 to a context, handles 1 to 32.
  The client owns the addresses below the `userRange` `createVM` returns, and the driver keeps the rest for its rings and sync objects.
: Buffer
  Pinned memory, up to 4 GiB, and 4 GiB all together in a context: write-combining, or, made with the `cpuCached` flag (1), mapped cached by the client for results it reads.
  A buffer's VMO can be given to another process, such as a display's driver (`CSFBuffer.exportVMO`): a new handle with the rights to be transferred, read and measured, not to be mapped or written, to pages the GPU wrote and the display reads as they are, since both are non-coherent with the CPU and neither needs its caches.
  A cached buffer is the GPU's non-cacheable memory all the same, which does not see the CPU's caches: its client cleans and invalidates the range (`VMO.cacheCleanInvalidate`) after the GPU has written it and before the CPU reads it, and after the CPU has written memory the GPU will write.
  The client maps its VMO; the driver binds ranges of it into a VM (4 KiB pages, 2 MiB where aligned; read-only, no-execute and uncached flags).
  A bind replaces what it overlaps, splitting it.
  A buffer let go of stays until nothing maps it.
: Group
  1 to 32 queues (and no more than the firmware's command-stream slots) over one VM; 128 to a context, handles 1 to 128.
  Each queue has a ring of 4 KiB to 64 KiB that only the driver writes.
  A group's priority is 0 or 1: high and realtime are refused, since no context holds the privilege.
  More groups than the firmware has slots run in turn.
: Submission
  Jobs, each a queue and a stream's address and size (64-byte aligned, a multiple of 8 bytes) in the VM, or no stream for a sync point.
  The driver writes the ring's sequence around the stream: a cache flush, the call, and a SYNC_ADD64 of the queue's sync object.
  A submission can wait on the client's counters, which it keeps in order with the group's other submissions, and raises its signals when its last job is done, whether the job ended well or not.
: Tiler heap
  The memory the tiler writes its bins into, which grows while a stream runs.
  `createHeap` takes a VM, the initial chunk count (at least 1), the chunk size (a multiple of 4 KiB, 128 KiB to 8 MiB), the most chunks the heap may ever hold (not fewer than the initial count) and the target in flight, and returns the heap's handle, the GPU address of its heap context and the address of its first chunk, which the client puts in its streams.
  A VM's heap contexts live in one pool buffer in the VM's kernel region, made with its first heap: 128 contexts of 32 bytes, each on the GPU's L2 cache line (64 bytes there, so 8 KiB), zeroed when the heap is made and the firmware's after that.
  A heap has up to 128 to a VM (the 129th gives `unavailable`); its handle is the VM's handle shifted up 16 bits, or the context's slot.
  Each chunk is a buffer of its own in the VM's kernel region, read/write and no-execute, never mapped by a client and never given a buffer handle.
  A chunk begins with a 64-byte header whose first word is the next chunk's address, with the chunk's size in 4 KiB units in its low 12 bits, or 0 at the end of the list.
  The initial chunks are linked so that the list the client gets runs from the last one made back to the first, and the first chunk reported is the last one made.
  A VM's heaps and pool take at most 2 GiB; `createHeap` past it, or without memory, gives `noMemory`.
  `destroyHeap` frees the heap's chunks and its context slot at once, without waiting for the GPU, so the client must have nothing queued or running that uses it: a group that does faults on the unmapped chunks and ends.
  A heap goes with its VM, once the VM's groups have left their slots, since the firmware may write a heap context while it suspends a group.

When a stream runs its heap out of memory the firmware raises TILER_OOM on that stream and mali, in the pass that takes the event, reads the heap context's address and the three pass counters (vertex-tiling started and finished, fragment finished).
It leaves the event unacknowledged while it decides: the counters must satisfy fragment-finished <= tiling-finished < tiling-started, and the address must be a live context of the group's VM pool, on a slot's start, else the stream is faulted fatally (the group ends as below) and nothing is acknowledged.
A valid request is granted one new chunk, with a zeroed header, when the passes in flight (started less fragment-finished) are no more than the heap's target and the heap holds fewer than its most chunks; the finished-tiling passes still waiting for their fragment work are not looked at.
Otherwise, or when no chunk can be had, it is refused.
The reply is the chunk's address with its size in 4 KiB units in the low 12 bits, or 0 for a refusal, written to both of the stream's heap registers; mali then acknowledges the event, toggles the stream's bit and rings the doorbell.
A refusal is not an error: the firmware waits for the fragment work to free memory, or runs the stream's own out-of-memory handler (incremental rendering, which is the client's), and only if neither works does the job fail and its queue fault.
A heap is never shrunk.
A group that has left its slot when the reply is due is not answered, and the chunk goes back; the firmware raises the event again when the group is resumed.

A group ends when a command stream faults fatally, the GPU's MMU faults on its VM, a job runs past 5 seconds, or a reset ends it: its jobs are cancelled and their signals raised, `groupState` says why, and the context's `submit` and `createGroup` fail with `lost`.
The context's other groups, and every other context, go on.

The memory the GPU reads is pinned memory the CPU does not cache, so it does no cache maintenance: a buffer is a VMO set write-combining and pinned a page at a time (the client maps the same VMO); one made `cpuCached` keeps the CPU's cache (cleaned and invalidated before it is pinned) and its client maintains it, as above.
The doorbell, and a client's submit, follow a complete barrier (`dsb`), so stores to write-combining memory are out before the GPU is told to look. and the driver's rings, sync objects, suspend buffers and page tables are pinned allocations of its own.
Each VM's page tables grow from pages of its own, up to 4 MiB of them, and go with the VM.
A VM takes one of the GPU's address spaces (slot 0 is the MCU's) while a group on it runs, and keeps it until another VM needs it, the one idle longest first.
A change to the tables of a VM that holds a slot is done under LOCK of the range, a flush of the GPU's caches and UNLOCK; a VM that holds none is changed plainly, and the next group on it programs the slot.
Each group has, in the MCU's address space, the two suspend buffers the firmware asks for and each queue's 8 KiB control area, and in its VM's own kernel region the queues' rings and their sync objects.

An MMU fault in a slot is read (the exception, the access, the address and the source are in `status`), cleared, and the slot disabled so that everything running on it faults; the group on it ends as above and the others go on.
A fatal error that stays with its stream (a bus fault on a call to an address nothing maps, say) and the MMU fault that goes with it end only that group and reset nothing.
These reset the whole GPU, the firmware loaded and booted again, groups that were suspended in time resuming and the rest ending:

- a ping not answered in 200 ms;
- a command-stream error of the kind `CS_UNRECOVERABLE`, after which the firmware's state can't be trusted;
- a group slot that acknowledges a state outside 0 to 3;
- a request to the firmware that is not acknowledged in 100 ms (a status update, a priority update, a group's start, suspend or terminate); a request not acknowledged within 0.5 ms is asked for again (see BUGS) before the 100 ms are up;
- an address-space command or a cache flush that is still busy after 100 ms.

The `event:` line that opens the reset says which: `GPU reset <n>: cause <why>`, the reasons of every request that was merged into it in the order they came, joined by `; `.
When devmgr(8) ends the GPU's supply, mali first asks the MCU to halt (by the interface's version: a request bit before 4.1, a state from it), waits up to 300 ms for it, and stops it, so the power is not cut under a running MCU; the line `mali: supply ending: ...` says how it went.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/mali-acpi-_SB_.GPU_`.

: `status`
  Read only.
  The firmware first: `firmware <size> bytes, <n> sections, git <revision>` (no revision when the image has none), `firmware <size> bytes refused: <rule>`, or `no firmware`.
  Then a line or more for each step done, in the order above, of these kinds:
  `Q-channel clock gating on, RCSU control <hex> harvest <hex>`;
  the GPU's name and GPU_ID and its fields (`Mali-G720-Immortalis, GPU_ID 0xc8700008: architecture 12.8.7, r0p0 status 8`), its cores (`shader cores 10 (0x00550555), tiler 0x00000001, L2 0x00000001`), the feature registers, and `probed`;
  `harvest: ...`, `soft reset: ...`, `coherency: ...`, `system cache: ...`, `L2 powered: ...`, `MCU VM: ...`, `firmware loaded: ...`, `address space 0: ...` and `MCU booted: ...`, each with the values it wrote or waited for;
  the firmware's interface: `firmware interface <major>.<minor>.<patch>, GLB_FEATURES <hex>`, then its slots: `<n> CSG slots (...), <m> CS slots each (CS_FEATURES <hex>: <r> work registers, <s> scoreboards, <kinds>)`;
  `GPU_TIMESTAMP counts at <hz> Hz`, `global interface configured: ...` and `ping answered, GLB_ACK <hex>`.
  On success the last lines are `budget name=gpu.mali.boot_ms value=<ms> unit=ms`, the time from the host's start to the firmware's answer to the ping, and `firmware booted`.
  Where a step failed, the last line is `FAILED at <step>: <why>`, the step one of `Q-channel clock gating`, `probe`, `firmware`, `harvest`, `soft reset`, `coherency`, `L2 power`, `MCU VM`, `firmware load`, `address space 0`, `MCU boot`, `interface` or `ping`.
  A GPU that was probed and then failed in a later step has no `firmware booted`.
  After it, from the running scheduler: `scheduler: JOB, MMU and GPU interrupts, JOB interrupts <n>, MMU faults <n>, resets <n>, GPU interrupts <n> (<n> faults)` (or `JOB and MMU interrupts; the GPU's line is polled with them: no interrupt (status <n>)`, or `polled every 2 ms: no interrupt for ...`); `interrupt JOB: <n> fired, <n> polls`, the MMU's and the GPU's; `address spaces: ` the first sixteen slots, `mcu`, `vm<n>` or `-`; the live buffers, VMs and groups; then the newest events, oldest first, each `event: ` (an MMU fault with its exception, access and address, a GPU-level fault, a reset and its cause, a control step that failed) or `firmware: ` (a command-stream error the firmware reported, a request it did not acknowledge, a ping it missed).
  Every event line opens with the time it was noted, as `[<ms> ms]`: milliseconds on the monotonic clock since the host started.
  The two kinds are each kept in order, and the times put one against the other.
  `tiler heaps: <n> in <m> VMs, <k> KiB (pools and chunks); OOM events <e>: granted <g>, refused <r>, invalid <i>` counts what the heaps hold and what their OOM events came to.
  The probe's lines include `threads <n> a core, workgroup <n>, barrier <n>; THREAD_FEATURES <hex>: <r> registers a core, <t> tasks a core`.

