# BUGS

The power-up is the Sky1's alone.
`power DEVICE off` and `on` in devmgr's `ctl` (devmgr(8)) cut the GPU and bring it back, each time with a new host that probes it, resets it and boots the firmware again; the halt before the cut is bounded at 300 ms and the host does not wait for the scheduler's lock, so a request in flight while it halts can be lost.
The GPU block's power-change events are not served: power is polled.
The firmware can drop a request to a group slot whose doorbell lands as the GPU goes idle (the global IDLE event is raised at that moment): it takes the doorbell and never reads the request until the next one; seen on status updates only, though every request shares the path, and why it drops it is not known.
A status update is therefore written only after the global events are acknowledged and a doorbell has been rung and seen, which removed every drop in the runs made.
Any request not answered in 0.5 ms is asked for again as a safety net: once the doorbell's acknowledge has caught up, its bit is toggled and the doorbell rung, every 0.5 ms until the 100 ms end in a reset.
The status line counts the requests that needed it (`requests asked again`) and the status updates sent in order (`ordered status updates`).
The `event:` and `firmware:` lines keep the newest 24 and 32; a long run has lost the oldest.
A reset's cause names the request that was seen, which is not always the first thing that went wrong: an address-space command that times out after a fault is named, and the fault is the line before it.
A tiler heap's chunks and pool are mapped like every GPU buffer, write-combining and uncached for the GPU, though the heap is meant cacheable.
The grant policy is the one the heap's limits make: a render that needs more memory than its heap's most chunks, or more passes in flight than its target, is left to its own incremental rendering, and a stream that has none ends with its job.
A chunk is made when the request is served, so a request waits for the memory to be pinned.
Every section and every buffer is mapped write-combining and uncached for the GPU, the MCU's code included, which is slower than the cached mapping the image asks for.
A section, a ring, a control area and a suspend buffer are each one contiguous allocation, so a very large one can fail for want of contiguous memory (`FAILED at firmware load`, or `createGroup` failing with `noMemory`); a client's buffer is pinned a page to an address and has no such limit.
Both suspend buffers are made for every group, though protected mode isn't used.
A buffer pinned for the GPU stays pinned until the last bind of it and its handle are gone.
The scheduler polls a submission that waits on a counter every 50 ms at most while the host is idle, since no thread is woken when a client raises one.
The firmware's timers are in GPU_TIMESTAMP ticks, whose rate is measured, not read from the system timer.
The GPU's own DMA, through the stub's BTI, reaches all of memory: nothing confines it beyond its own page tables.
